# Standard application answers

Reusable answers to the questions almost every application form asks. Adapt the
wording per company, don't paste verbatim.

## Factual, no adaptation needed

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| Notice period | None. Available from January 2027, after December 2026 graduation. |
| Visa / work authorisation | UAE resident. **No sponsorship required.** |
| Willing to relocate | Within the UAE, yes. |
| Right to work in UAE | Yes. |
| Languages | Arabic (native), English (fluent). |
| Earliest start date | January 2027. Available for part-time or project work before then. |
| Referred by anyone | Check `outreach-log.md` first. |

## Salary expectation

**STILL NEEDS ADEL'S INPUT. Do not invent a number.**

Asked 12 Sept 2026. His answer: *"idk if they ask ill think abt it then."* So the
standing instruction is: write "negotiable" where the form allows free text, and
**stop and ask him** on any form that hard-requires a figure. Do not fill it in.

Reference points for when he does decide: Nexus Consultancy published
AED 15,000–18,000/month for a Dubai cybersecurity presales role requiring 2 years.
Duavel offered AED 4,000/month for an AI/ML role requiring 3 years, which is an
outlier and should not anchor anything.

If a form requires one and this is still blank, stop and ask him. A number
guessed wrong here is expensive in both directions, and it is the one field on an
application that cannot be walked back.

Once he gives a range, record it here with the date, and note whether it is
monthly or annual and whether it is all-in or basic. UAE packages are usually
quoted monthly and all-in.

## Why this company

Don't reuse an answer here. Write it per company, and ground it in something
specific and checkable: a product, a market position, something they published.
Generic enthusiasm reads as generic.

Reusable true framing: he chose an infrastructure company for his internship
specifically to see the whole stack rather than one layer, and he wants an
employer where the technical work connects to a commercial outcome he can see.

## Why this role

Pull from `about-me.md`. The honest and unusual version:

> I arrived at Dell closed off to commercial roles and expecting to stay purely
> technical. Mapping how the business actually works changed that. The direction
> I settled on is to start deeply technical, move toward solutions or consulting
> specialised in security, because moving from technical into commercial is
> straightforward and the reverse is very hard.

## Greatest strength

Self-direction, and it is evidenced rather than claimed: 12 projects in 10 weeks,
none assigned, all sourced by identifying a specialist and asking for scope.

## Greatest weakness / a failure

Use the week-seven presentation. Sound work, poor delivery, because he had never
said the material out loud. He wrote a retrospective naming five root causes,
applied the corrections within one week, and re-pitched successfully within two.
Do not soften this. The arc is what makes it a good answer, and most graduate
candidates cannot answer this question with a real one.

## Cover letter shape

Only write one if the posting asks. Three short paragraphs:

1. The specific thing about this role or company. Never "I am writing to apply".
2. One concrete project with its real numbers, chosen to match the posting.
3. Availability from January 2027, no sponsorship needed, and a clear close.

Keep it under 250 words. His written voice is plain and direct: short sentences,
concrete numbers, no adjectives doing work that facts should do.
